1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,600
U.S. bombers and missiles.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,600
America's Cold War nuclear arsenal.

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,600
The most powerful military force ever created.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,300
Now, the Strategic Air Command on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,400
For over 45 years during the Cold War, an all-encompassing military machine controlled

6
00:00:37,401 --> 00:00:40,040
America's nuclear weapons.

7
00:00:40,041 --> 00:00:45,280
Some quarter of a million people strong, the Strategic Air Command was known by its acronym,

8
00:00:45,281 --> 00:00:46,280
SAC.

9
00:00:46,281 --> 00:00:52,200
The men and women of the command did not make foreign policy, but trained to be its ultimate

10
00:00:52,201 --> 00:00:54,200
enforcer.

11
00:00:54,201 --> 00:01:01,100
They did not build the weapons, but masterminded how they were deployed and where they would strike.

12
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:06,640
They did not defend the country, but by their sheer strength deterred the enemy from attack.

13
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:15,520
It was a proud command, but it was a disciplined command with a heavy boot.

14
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:18,240
And discipline was the name of the game.

15
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:21,660
And I'm glad, because it needed to be disciplined.

16
00:01:21,661 --> 00:01:25,340
It was dealing with things that were so important and so critical.

17
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:27,860
You couldn't make a mistake.

18
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:33,860
The heart of the United States nuclear forces was a triad of military might.

19
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:38,440
SAC bombers and refueling aircraft made up the first prong of the attack.

20
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,860
SAC intercontinental ballistic missiles located throughout the U.S.

21
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,380
were another system ready at a moment's notice to decimate enemy targets.

22
00:01:48,381 --> 00:01:54,664
while the Navy's nuclear missile-bearing submarines were the third and final sea-based arm of the U.S.

23
00:01:54,665 --> 00:01:55,365
offensive.

24
00:01:56,340 --> 00:02:01,560
Reconnaissance Air and spacecraft, as well as mobile command centers, rounded out SAC's capability.

25
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:08,020
At its height, the Strategic Air Command controlled over 3,000 aircraft,

26
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:12,460
thousands of missiles, and tens of thousands of nuclear weapons.

27
00:02:12,461 --> 00:02:19,460
The command's emblem was a raised armored fist holding both olive branch and lightning bolts.

28
00:02:19,461 --> 00:02:25,460
Sack's message was clear. Peace through absolute strength.

29
00:02:25,461 --> 00:02:34,460
During the Cold War, that strength hinged on the military men and women of the command, rationally facing the unthinkable.

30
00:02:34,461 --> 00:02:47,320
I can't imagine any more challenging career than preparing, training, working as hard as you could to do a job that you hoped you never, ever had the opportunity to do.

31
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:56,675
How do you wake up in the morning and punch your three-year-old daughter on your knee, kiss her goodbye, go to work, and realize that if you do what you're trained

32
00:02:56,676 --> 00:02:59,000
for, she may not be there when you get home?

33
00:02:59,001 --> 00:03:08,260
With the ironic motto, peace is our profession, the United States Strategic Air Command was a military giant.

34
00:03:08,261 --> 00:03:16,900
It's probably as an effective, a military enterprise, if you will, that the world has ever known.

35
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:27,400
It operated automatically, meticulously, nothing's ever error-free in the military, but it really was an awesome force.

36
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,760
So horrible were the consequences of using these weapons that military leaders and politicians

37
00:03:35,761 --> 00:03:41,120
alike focused on their ability to stop a war before it started.

38
00:03:41,121 --> 00:03:45,080
Deterrence in its simplest form means that I have a certain set of capabilities that

39
00:03:45,081 --> 00:03:49,620
will be so devastating to you that you won't take actions that force me to use it.

40
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:50,960
You won't attack me, for example.

41
00:03:50,961 --> 00:03:52,880
You won't attack my allies.

42
00:03:52,881 --> 00:03:53,880
You won't attack my homeland.

43
00:03:53,881 --> 00:03:56,000
You won't attack my vital interests.

44
00:03:56,001 --> 00:04:03,000
But a century ago, deterrence backed by such speed and certainty not only did not exist,

45
00:04:03,001 --> 00:04:05,000
it hadn't even been thought of.

46
00:04:05,001 --> 00:04:11,000
The creation of fixed-wing aircraft at the beginning of the 20th century changed warfare forever.

47
00:04:11,001 --> 00:04:18,000
Before airplanes, destroying an enemy's strategic target, such as weapons makers and food supplies,

48
00:04:18,001 --> 00:04:21,000
required first defeating its army on the battlefield.

49
00:04:21,001 --> 00:04:27,760
With airplanes, forces were able to fly over enemy lines and strike at the heart of their

50
00:04:27,761 --> 00:04:28,760
opponent's war machine.

51
00:04:28,761 --> 00:04:35,560
First of all, you have to go back to World War I, and the United States really didn't

52
00:04:35,561 --> 00:04:36,560
have any airplanes.

53
00:04:36,561 --> 00:04:40,740
They had to beg, borrow, and steal French and British airplanes to fly.

54
00:04:40,741 --> 00:04:47,780
And the concept of strategic bombardment was developed by the Germans, the French, the

55
00:04:47,781 --> 00:04:52,651
The Russians, the Italians, the British, and the U.S.

56
00:04:52,652 --> 00:04:55,960
really didn't get into it until much

57
00:04:55,961 --> 00:04:57,800
later.

58
00:04:57,801 --> 00:05:02,720
Air power visionaries such as Brigadier General William Billy Mitchell saw in the airplane

59
00:05:02,721 --> 00:05:05,720
a brave new strategic force.

60
00:05:05,721 --> 00:05:10,500
But inaccurate targeting with the early weapons caused most in the military to view the technology

61
00:05:10,501 --> 00:05:13,840
with skepticism.

62
00:05:13,841 --> 00:05:18,489
bombardment starting back in the early days. They were not wrong.

63
00:05:18,490 --> 00:05:19,920
They were just about

64
00:05:19,921 --> 00:05:26,500
50 years or 75 years ahead of their time. After the First World War, the science of

65
00:05:26,501 --> 00:05:29,958
finding a target and striking it with a bomb improved.

66
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:32,200
For early bombardiers, weapons that

67
00:05:32,201 --> 00:05:37,220
were aerodynamically shaped and the addition of better bomb sites in the aircraft were great

68
00:05:37,221 --> 00:05:41,620
improvements. American pilots were getting better at their destructive craft.

69
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:49,140
During the early 1920s, the fledgling Army Air Corps attempted to prove aerial bombardment's

70
00:05:49,141 --> 00:05:54,220
effectiveness against a captured and gutted German battleship called the Ostfriesland.

71
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:58,146
With Navy officials watching, U.S.

72
00:05:58,147 --> 00:06:01,940
bombers repeatedly struck and eventually sank the ship.

73
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:03,500
Still, U.S.

74
00:06:03,501 --> 00:06:08,480
military leaders decided to ignore the tests and stick with what they knew best,

75
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:10,680
armies and ships.

76
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:14,380
That decision would eventually haunt the U.S.

77
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:23,180
On December 7, 1941, at Pearl Harbor,

78
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,760
363 Japanese warplanes flew in undetected over the island of Oahu.

79
00:06:28,761 --> 00:06:34,820
When the attack was over, five American battleships were destroyed and the base lay in ruin.

80
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:40,360
All questions regarding aerial bombardment's effectiveness were answered by the sunken ships.

81
00:06:42,620 --> 00:06:46,180
For the next five years, Japan and America were at war.

82
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:48,721
In that time, the U.S.

83
00:06:48,722 --> 00:06:53,720
Army's Air Force dropped nearly 153,000 tons of bombs on its enemy.

84
00:06:53,721 --> 00:06:59,580
Three and a half years into the war in the Pacific, the man who would become SAC's most influential commander,

85
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:04,900
General Curtis Emerson LeMay, took command of the 20th Air Force Pacific bomber fleet.

86
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:10,480
Gruff, hardworking, and controversial, LeMay was rewriting the book on strategic bombardment.

87
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:21,660
He did things like lowering our fuel reserves, taking the armor plate out of the B-29s so we could carry more bombs.

88
00:07:21,661 --> 00:07:26,660
It scared some of the crews, the air crews.

89
00:07:26,661 --> 00:07:30,660
If we didn't have the armor plate, we didn't have as much fuel reserve,

90
00:07:30,661 --> 00:07:35,660
and we didn't have as many rounds of ammunition on our machine guns,

91
00:07:35,661 --> 00:07:38,660
we just thought it would be the end.

92
00:07:38,661 --> 00:07:43,660
But as we did these things, we found out we were carrying more bombs.

93
00:07:43,661 --> 00:07:45,660
We were putting them on the target more accurately.

94
00:07:45,661 --> 00:07:55,660
But in the mountains of New Mexico, American scientists were developing a weapon that depended less on accuracy than brute force.

95
00:07:58,660 --> 00:08:07,660
On July 16, 1945, physicists working on the top secret Manhattan Project succeeded in creating a catastrophic nuclear blast.

96
00:08:07,661 --> 00:08:09,660
catastrophic nuclear blast.

97
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:17,660
The Manhattan Project developed atomic weapons.

98
00:08:17,661 --> 00:08:20,660
We had two of them go to Japan,

99
00:08:20,661 --> 00:08:22,660
with a little boy and a fat man.

100
00:08:22,661 --> 00:08:26,660
And they dropped the bombs of Nagasaki and Hiroshima

101
00:08:26,661 --> 00:08:30,660
and literally brought the war to the end

102
00:08:30,661 --> 00:08:34,660
because it broke the will of the enemy to continue.

103
00:08:34,661 --> 00:08:41,660
The war that had begun with hundreds of bombs at Pearl Harbor ended with two detonating over Japan.

104
00:08:41,661 --> 00:08:48,660
The United States and the world had entered the nuclear age.

105
00:08:48,661 --> 00:08:54,660
I'd be uncomfortable stating from the beginning that we viewed it as just any other weapon.

106
00:08:54,661 --> 00:09:01,660
But on the other hand, it took us probably by the 50s to really internalize the nature of a revolutionary weapon.

107
00:09:01,661 --> 00:09:05,920
revolutionary weapon most understood that they constitute a revolutionary new

108
00:09:05,921 --> 00:09:10,540
development military technology and by that definition it takes time for

109
00:09:10,541 --> 00:09:15,700
societies for military organizations for national leaders in many countries to

110
00:09:15,701 --> 00:09:20,080
really internalize what those weapons mean both to policy to capabilities to

111
00:09:20,081 --> 00:09:26,020
peace and to security while politicians grappled with the policies surrounding

112
00:09:26,021 --> 00:09:30,400
nuclear weapons the military began discussing just how they fit in the

113
00:09:30,401 --> 00:09:34,740
future U.S. arsenal, a debate that intensified as a new threat loomed.

114
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:42,980
Next, an old ally becomes a new enemy, and the Strategic Air Command is born.

115
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:50,358
Many believe the first strategic bomber was the Russian-built Ilya Moromets.

116
00:09:50,359 --> 00:09:51,600
Constructed in 1913,

117
00:09:51,601 --> 00:09:57,260
the four-engine plane boasted a range of nearly 400 miles and carried over a half-ton of bombs.

118
00:09:57,261 --> 00:10:01,260
The Strategic Air Command will return on Modern Marvels.

119
00:10:01,261 --> 00:10:05,260
We now return to the Strategic Air Command on Modern Marvels.

120
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:11,260
In August of 1945, America was tired of war.

121
00:10:11,261 --> 00:10:16,260
Having spent the previous five years leading the Allied victory over both Germany and Japan,

122
00:10:16,261 --> 00:10:20,260
the United States was happy to begin dismantling its forces.

123
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:26,260
But the stewardship of devastating nuclear weapons was a problematic responsibility.

124
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:27,960
The U.S.

125
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:34,380
military began focusing on a strategic way that would oversee the new bombs and provide

126
00:10:34,381 --> 00:10:36,500
the means by which they were delivered.

127
00:10:36,501 --> 00:10:39,620
It was the end of World War II.

128
00:10:39,621 --> 00:10:40,880
Nobody thought it was going to be needed.

129
00:10:40,881 --> 00:10:46,320
But SAC was formed because we had a lot of big airplanes and we had a capability to go

130
00:10:46,321 --> 00:10:48,320
longer distances.

131
00:10:48,321 --> 00:10:52,980
They thought they ought to put it together in a ball of wax and let it run around and

132
00:10:52,981 --> 00:10:58,420
practice going long distances and delivering weapons just in case it's needed.

133
00:10:58,421 --> 00:11:07,020
From such inauspicious beginnings, the Strategic Air Command was created on March 21, 1946.

134
00:11:07,021 --> 00:11:13,820
Karl Tuohy Spatz, Commanding General of the Army's Air Forces, defined SAC's purpose.

135
00:11:13,821 --> 00:11:19,520
To conduct long-range offensive operations, maximum range reconnaissance, and provide combat

136
00:11:19,521 --> 00:11:23,920
units able to employ the latest most advanced weapons.

137
00:11:23,921 --> 00:11:29,740
Training personnel to maintain the force was also part of the job.

138
00:11:29,741 --> 00:11:35,860
Within six months, SAC was under control of the newly created US Air Force, but not before

139
00:11:35,861 --> 00:11:41,140
General Spatz chose the Strategic Air Command's first leader.

140
00:11:41,141 --> 00:11:45,100
General George C. Kenney became the first commander of Strategic Air Command.

141
00:11:45,101 --> 00:11:52,800
He spent a lot of his time on the political circuit trying to drive for a separate Air Force.

142
00:11:53,880 --> 00:11:57,000
And SAC really didn't perform very well during that period.

143
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:03,100
An additional hindrance, SAC had been chosen to wield America's nuclear weapons,

144
00:12:03,420 --> 00:12:05,100
but it wasn't allowed to have any.

145
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:08,492
By 1948, all 50 nuclear bombs in the U.S.

146
00:12:08,493 --> 00:12:11,920
arsenal remained under the control of their builders,

147
00:12:11,921 --> 00:12:13,580
the Atomic Energy Commission.

148
00:12:13,581 --> 00:12:20,580
SAC would have to wait until the early 50s before having the weapons at its immediate disposal.

149
00:12:20,581 --> 00:12:26,580
But increasing world tensions were about to give SAC a new importance.

150
00:12:26,581 --> 00:12:36,580
By the late 1940s, the political climate between the US, England and France, and their one-time ally, the Soviet Union, was cooling.

151
00:12:36,581 --> 00:12:40,580
The first point of contention was the division of Berlin.

152
00:12:40,581 --> 00:12:49,920
In June of 1948, Soviet leaders, enraged by what they saw as an unfair division of the German capital, cut access to the western portion of the city.

153
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:53,620
They were attempting to starve the West into submission.

154
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:56,050
For the next year, U.S.

155
00:12:56,051 --> 00:13:04,880
and European air crews flew over a quarter million flights to the city, delivering nearly 2.5 million tons of food, coal, and other necessities.

156
00:13:05,740 --> 00:13:06,906
In charge of the U.S.

157
00:13:06,907 --> 00:13:11,960
portion of the mission, former wartime Pacific bomber fleet commander General Curtis LeMay.

158
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:18,840
Curtis LeMay, whether you love him or hate him, no one would say that he wasn't probably the true definition of a leader.

159
00:13:19,340 --> 00:13:24,980
And that any time you make tough decisions that will impact the lives of individuals but also of nations,

160
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:28,840
there are going to be people who agree with how you do it and people who disagree with how you do it.

161
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:34,360
The man who had helped write the book on strategic bombing and supervised the Berlin airlift

162
00:13:34,361 --> 00:13:36,320
seemed a perfect match for SAC.

163
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,520
On October 15, 1948,

164
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:42,180
the Air Force put General LeMay in charge

165
00:13:42,181 --> 00:13:44,120
of the lackluster Strategic Air Command.

166
00:13:45,260 --> 00:13:47,320
With Berlin still fresh in his memory,

167
00:13:47,860 --> 00:13:50,680
LeMay considered the Soviets a major threat to the U.S.

168
00:13:50,681 --> 00:13:53,280
and believed his new command would be instrumental

169
00:13:53,281 --> 00:13:54,480
in keeping them at bay.

170
00:13:55,140 --> 00:13:58,640
His hopes met reality upon his first inspection of SAC forces.

171
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:01,240
He claimed disgustedly,

172
00:14:01,241 --> 00:14:04,900
I found a soldier guarding a hangar with a ham sandwich.

173
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:10,440
Of even more concern, Sack had no plan of attack in the event of war.

174
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:12,880
No routes plotted, nor cities targeted.

175
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:16,180
LeMay vowed to remake Sack any way he could.

176
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,820
One of his solutions was to make sure his best people moved up quickly.

177
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:24,640
He wanted to motivate his people and his air crews.

178
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:27,700
And he developed what was known as a spot promotion program.

179
00:14:27,701 --> 00:14:36,180
The ones that won the bombing competitions, around the world flight, something of great import, the whole crew was elevated one grade.

180
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:43,488
All you had to do was have one guy screw up on that crew, and the whole crew was busted back.

181
00:14:43,489 --> 00:14:44,600
He was a stickler.

182
00:14:45,660 --> 00:14:49,600
Of more importance, LeMay defined SAC's strategic nuclear war plan.

183
00:14:49,601 --> 00:14:58,880
It called for attacks on 70 Soviet cities with 133 atomic bombs over the course of 30 days.

184
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:05,240
In later years, some in the popular press pegged LeMay as a warmonger.

185
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:10,300
Phrases such as, bomb them back to the Stone Age, were attributed to him.

186
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:17,840
Still, he oversaw Sack's change from a second-rate outpost to one of the most respected commands in the U.S.

187
00:15:17,841 --> 00:15:18,541
military,

188
00:15:18,542 --> 00:15:21,560
a transformation which faced some unlikely resistance.

189
00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:28,140
An almost irrational fear of communism left Congress willing to spend billions on the military.

190
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:31,520
But the money wasn't limitless.

191
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:37,480
The biggest opponents to the Strategic Air Command spending were often other military branches.

192
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:45,140
In the late 40s, LeMay testified before Congress on the need to build a fleet of new long-range bombers.

193
00:15:45,141 --> 00:15:50,140
The Navy had been seeking to procure the same funds for a supercarrier.

194
00:15:50,141 --> 00:15:57,140
A bitter fight ensued, with the Navy claiming fraud and referring to the new bomber as the

195
00:15:57,141 --> 00:15:59,140
billion-dollar blunder.

196
00:15:59,141 --> 00:16:04,140
Still, Congress sided with the Air Force, and SAC received its B-36 bomber.

197
00:16:04,141 --> 00:16:11,140
With a wingspan of 230 feet, the enormous B-36 was the largest American bomber ever

198
00:16:11,141 --> 00:16:12,140
built.

199
00:16:12,141 --> 00:16:18,600
With its 72,000-pound payload, it could carry both conventional and nuclear weapons.

200
00:16:19,980 --> 00:16:27,860
The development of the B-36 and the capability for us to send a bomber from South Dakota or from Nebraska

201
00:16:27,861 --> 00:16:34,720
all the way around the world to the Soviet Union, drop a payload and return without refueling, was an incredible feat.

202
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:39,440
But LeMay's victory was bittersweet. The Navy had been right.

203
00:16:39,441 --> 00:16:44,340
The mammoth B-36 was grossly inefficient.

204
00:16:44,341 --> 00:16:49,280
Propeller driven, it was slow, and most of the space on the plane was reserved for fuel,

205
00:16:49,281 --> 00:16:50,280
not bombs.

206
00:16:50,281 --> 00:16:56,880
It had been built on the premise that a bomber can't pull over and stop for gas.

207
00:16:56,881 --> 00:17:01,660
But that's exactly what SAC needed, aerial gas stations.

208
00:17:01,661 --> 00:17:07,280
One of many war technology advancements was in-flight refueling.

209
00:17:07,281 --> 00:17:14,280
The KC-97 tanker would cater to a developing breed of new jet bombers, which could refuel en route to their target.

210
00:17:14,281 --> 00:17:21,280
SAC would soon boast an efficient and sustainable global reach without the Ponderous B-36s.

211
00:17:21,281 --> 00:17:24,280
Meanwhile, the new enemy became stronger.

212
00:17:24,281 --> 00:17:31,280
On August 29, 1949, the Soviet Union detonated their first atomic bomb.

213
00:17:31,281 --> 00:17:43,000
Three years later, on October 31, 1952, America upped the stakes by detonating the first hydrogen

214
00:17:43,001 --> 00:17:44,000
bomb.

215
00:17:44,001 --> 00:17:51,500
In around 1950 and so, there was even the notion of a preventive war, mostly in Air Force

216
00:17:51,501 --> 00:17:58,520
circles, that we had this fundamental confrontation with the Soviet Union.

217
00:17:58,521 --> 00:18:02,440
We were going to have to fight a war with them, and we ought to do it while we're ahead.

218
00:18:03,260 --> 00:18:05,440
But the Soviets were catching up fast.

219
00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:09,800
In August of 1953, by exploding their own hydrogen bomb,

220
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:13,000
Russia dampened American thoughts of a preventive war.

221
00:18:15,020 --> 00:18:18,400
But America's darkest Cold War days were still to come.

222
00:18:18,401 --> 00:18:23,500
On the 15th of May, 1957, the Soviets started tests on what would become

223
00:18:23,501 --> 00:18:26,260
the world's first intercontinental ballistic missile.

224
00:18:26,261 --> 00:18:32,040
Unlike bombers, the device had the ability to deliver a nuclear warhead in minutes as opposed to hours.

225
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:35,780
The visionaries saw that by putting SAC in the middle of the country,

226
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:38,940
you made it a more difficult target for the enemy.

227
00:18:39,780 --> 00:18:43,541
The Air Force moved the command's headquarters from Washington, D.C.

228
00:18:43,542 --> 00:18:45,200
to the dead center of America.

229
00:18:46,940 --> 00:18:52,880
Its new headquarters on Offutt Air Force Base in Nebraska was built as an underground bunker fortification.

230
00:18:52,881 --> 00:18:59,880
Dug from the fertile Nebraska plains, 25 feet below this yellow brick military building,

231
00:18:59,881 --> 00:19:05,880
sits another three-story structure with nearly 130,000 square feet of sheltered floor space.

232
00:19:05,881 --> 00:19:12,880
For the rest of the Cold War, the command post housed top-secret map rooms and communication equipment.

233
00:19:12,881 --> 00:19:18,880
Here, commanders tracked forces and performed administrative duties for SAC bases around the world.

234
00:19:18,881 --> 00:19:19,581
world.

235
00:19:19,582 --> 00:19:25,120
With walls nearly three feet thick, blast doors and its own artesian well, the

236
00:19:25,121 --> 00:19:29,338
facility was designed to survive a near hit by a nuclear weapon.

237
00:19:29,339 --> 00:19:30,920
But even more important,

238
00:19:30,921 --> 00:19:33,879
it was built with the idea that it was expendable.

239
00:19:33,880 --> 00:19:36,660
SAC's best defense was the fact that its forces

240
00:19:36,661 --> 00:19:41,600
and command structure were being dispersed throughout the US and Europe.

241
00:19:41,601 --> 00:19:45,535
Our command was structured so that it could take damage and still operate.

242
00:19:45,536 --> 00:19:46,280
We had several

243
00:19:46,281 --> 00:19:51,280
locations that had all of the capabilities of the headquarters.

244
00:19:51,281 --> 00:19:56,040
Multiple bases ensured that pieces could be lost to a Russian nuclear strike and SAC would

245
00:19:56,041 --> 00:19:58,040
still carry out its mission.

246
00:19:58,041 --> 00:20:04,760
The strategy proved necessary as the Soviet Union continued to fly farther and higher.

247
00:20:04,761 --> 00:20:12,420
On October 4, 1957, six months after the Soviets began testing their ICBMs, Sputnik, the first

248
00:20:12,421 --> 00:20:16,080
artificial satellite to orbit the Earth, was launched.

249
00:20:16,081 --> 00:20:20,500
was now behind in a race in which winning or losing could mean the end of the world.

250
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:22,540
The U.S.

251
00:20:22,541 --> 00:20:26,600
Strategic Air Command was charged with playing catch-up, a task that would turn

252
00:20:26,601 --> 00:20:30,800
the command into the largest and most dangerous fighting force the world had ever seen.

253
00:20:32,060 --> 00:20:37,340
Next, SAC expands its role to keep an eye on the Soviet Union, and pilots trade in their

254
00:20:37,341 --> 00:20:39,700
home in the sky for a hole in the ground.

255
00:20:39,701 --> 00:20:47,940
A SAC B-50 bomber made the first non-stop around the world flight.

256
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:52,140
The 1949 trip took 94 hours.

257
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:56,440
The Strategic Air Command will return on Modern Marvels.

258
00:20:57,760 --> 00:21:01,580
We now return to the Strategic Air Command on Modern Marvels.

259
00:21:01,581 --> 00:21:14,400
By 1957, SAC commanders were in charge of an operation over 220,000 people strong.

260
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:22,700
That same year, Curtis LeMay left the Strategic Air Command after nearly nine years when he was promoted to Air Force Vice Chief of Staff.

261
00:21:23,700 --> 00:21:29,340
No other commander would spend as much time in charge, nor have as profound an impact on SAC.

262
00:21:29,341 --> 00:21:36,140
Under the shadow of the ever-growing Soviet threat, Congress raced to greatly increase

263
00:21:36,141 --> 00:21:38,260
military spending.

264
00:21:38,261 --> 00:21:43,340
The money started rolling in for the Air Force and for the Strategic Air Command.

265
00:21:43,341 --> 00:21:50,840
By the late 50s, SAC posted 2,711 aircraft, 200 of which were a new breed of bomber developed

266
00:21:50,841 --> 00:21:52,840
by Boeing, the B-52.

267
00:21:52,841 --> 00:21:59,800
The B-52 and the development of that aircraft really has revolutionized our nation's military

268
00:21:59,801 --> 00:22:02,040
and certainly revolutionized strategic bombing.

269
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:05,600
And you look at an aircraft that essentially is the same.

270
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:08,780
The instrumentation has changed, the people have changed,

271
00:22:09,040 --> 00:22:13,420
but the physical aircraft itself is the same today as it was when it first came off the assembly line.

272
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:17,120
Between 1955 and 1963,

273
00:22:17,121 --> 00:22:23,480
744 of the eight-engine monsters were created at Boeing's Wichita and Seattle plants.

274
00:22:23,980 --> 00:22:26,980
Each aircraft was able to carry 70,000 pounds of weapons.

275
00:22:27,800 --> 00:22:33,780
The plane behind me is a B-52, which was a staple bomber of the Strategic Air Command.

276
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:39,200
And this is the bomb bay of the B-52, and as you can see, it's quite cavernous.

277
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:43,260
We could carry, in the conventional mode, up to 108 bombs.

278
00:22:43,261 --> 00:22:47,200
and in the nuclear mode, we can carry four nuclear weapons in here.

279
00:22:49,740 --> 00:22:53,680
By the late 1950s, these planes, as well as other jet bombers,

280
00:22:53,820 --> 00:22:59,000
were flying regular training missions from SAC bases in Newfoundland, Labrador, Greenland,

281
00:22:59,500 --> 00:23:02,620
North Africa, England, Spain, and Guam.

282
00:23:04,820 --> 00:23:08,060
The crew's flight paths encircled Soviet airspace.

283
00:23:08,600 --> 00:23:12,000
The new bases have been built to get SAC forces closer to the enemy.

284
00:23:13,260 --> 00:23:18,260
Defining their potential targets became a priority for SAC.

285
00:23:18,261 --> 00:23:23,260
The late 50s saw the development of a new strategic air command mission.

286
00:23:23,261 --> 00:23:32,260
US military and political leaders caught off guard by Sputnik decided intelligence gathering was as important as nuclear weapons and bombers.

287
00:23:32,261 --> 00:23:41,260
The U-2 spy planes and later the SR-71 Blackbird were housed at Beale Air Force Base just north of San Francisco, California.

288
00:23:43,260 --> 00:23:49,900
A cooperative venture between SAC and the CIA, the highly secretive planes flew reconnaissance

289
00:23:49,901 --> 00:23:52,560
missions over the Soviet Union.

290
00:23:52,561 --> 00:23:58,340
From an altitude of 80,000 feet and reaching speeds of over Mach 3, about 2,100 miles per

291
00:23:58,341 --> 00:24:03,060
hour, the SR-71 was all but untouchable.

292
00:24:03,061 --> 00:24:04,176
But the U.S.

293
00:24:04,177 --> 00:24:11,340
had been developing the ability to fly higher and faster than even the SR-71.

294
00:24:11,341 --> 00:24:16,340
During the late 50s, SAC began testing its own intercontinental ballistic missiles.

295
00:24:16,341 --> 00:24:21,500
Remember it takes bombers hours and hours and hours to go from the United States to

296
00:24:21,501 --> 00:24:24,480
the Soviet Union or from the Soviet Union to the United States.

297
00:24:24,481 --> 00:24:27,980
We slowly begin to shift over toward land-based missiles.

298
00:24:27,981 --> 00:24:29,060
The reason was twofold.

299
00:24:29,061 --> 00:24:34,920
One is by putting it on missiles, we shorten that reaction time from hours to minutes.

300
00:24:34,921 --> 00:24:39,060
The second is that once we started moving in this direction, it began to push the technology

301
00:24:39,061 --> 00:24:41,560
to make nuclear weapons smaller, much more accurate.

302
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:44,920
On September 9, 1959,

303
00:24:45,640 --> 00:24:49,360
SAC launched its first true ICBM, the Atlas missile.

304
00:24:51,040 --> 00:24:54,700
The 85-foot-tall, three-engine, liquid-fueled rocket

305
00:24:54,701 --> 00:24:57,580
was able to reach an altitude of 900 miles

306
00:24:57,581 --> 00:24:59,700
before dropping back to Earth with its payload.

307
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:02,840
When they started up the Atlas E program,

308
00:25:03,340 --> 00:25:04,980
we were all in a 320th bomb wing,

309
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:07,240
and they deactivated the 320th bomb wing,

310
00:25:07,241 --> 00:25:10,040
and he took all the pilots and sent them to Atlas.

311
00:25:13,080 --> 00:25:16,020
We used to say, out of the sky and into a hole in the ground.

312
00:25:16,620 --> 00:25:18,300
But we knew that SAC needed it.

313
00:25:18,620 --> 00:25:20,480
The Russians were already putting things in the ground

314
00:25:20,481 --> 00:25:22,900
and we needed something to counterbalance that.

315
00:25:23,460 --> 00:25:25,420
And it was important, so we did it.

316
00:25:25,880 --> 00:25:26,880
And we did it very well.

317
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:30,660
But the Atlas system had a fatal flaw.

318
00:25:31,300 --> 00:25:33,520
The rocket was designed to be stored empty,

319
00:25:33,521 --> 00:25:35,420
which meant in the event of an attack,

320
00:25:35,421 --> 00:25:38,740
ground crews would spend precious time fueling the rocket.

321
00:25:40,460 --> 00:25:43,080
While other missiles would join the SAC inventory.

322
00:25:43,820 --> 00:25:48,520
It was the Minuteman missile that represented a huge leap forward in military technology.

323
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:53,040
Always fueled and stored in underground hardened silos with retractable doors.

324
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:55,460
It could be launched at a moment's notice.

325
00:25:56,740 --> 00:26:02,420
The Minuteman also held up to three warheads, meaning that each missile was a multiple threat.

326
00:26:02,421 --> 00:26:06,680
keeping them safe from attack was a top priority

327
00:26:06,681 --> 00:26:11,980
Air Force planners built the silos and control centers far enough apart

328
00:26:11,981 --> 00:26:15,220
so they couldn't be destroyed by a single nuclear blast

329
00:26:15,221 --> 00:26:19,020
In some cases the control centers were at such a great distance

330
00:26:19,021 --> 00:26:21,840
that crews traveled hours to get to their posts

331
00:26:21,841 --> 00:26:25,240
Security personnel checked each crew that entered the site

332
00:26:26,240 --> 00:26:27,980
Yes sir, can I have your team chief?

333
00:26:28,060 --> 00:26:31,320
Trip number, number additional personnel and has your vehicle been searched?

334
00:26:31,321 --> 00:26:35,940
Trip 16, Captain Martin plus one vehicle has been searched.

335
00:26:35,941 --> 00:26:43,840
The bomber crews and tanker crews had a little bit more freedom that they could move around on the base and alert vehicles and so forth.

336
00:26:44,280 --> 00:26:52,780
But your missile crew members were on alert in a missile silo in a launch control facility and locked down in the capsule.

337
00:26:54,400 --> 00:27:00,400
Strict procedures and missile silo design features were implemented to ensure against an accidental launch.

338
00:27:01,320 --> 00:27:07,920
Two missile launch officers needed to simultaneously operate controls at separate consoles to launch an ICBM.

339
00:27:09,400 --> 00:27:15,520
From their seats within the control center, the two missileers waited to receive encoded launch orders.

340
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:20,280
If the code arrived, it was cross-checked with launch codes in the control center.

341
00:27:20,720 --> 00:27:23,260
Alpha, zero, angle.

342
00:27:23,261 --> 00:27:32,000
If the message was correct, the two missileers simultaneously turned keys on their console, which activated the silo door and launched the missile.

343
00:27:32,920 --> 00:27:36,406
But the Air Force, which was in charge of SAC, wasn't the only U.S.

344
00:27:36,407 --> 00:27:38,800
military branch working with nuclear missiles.

345
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:41,293
In the late 50s, the U.S.

346
00:27:41,294 --> 00:27:48,460
Navy, thanks to smaller nuclear warheads and solid fuel technology, began deploying submarine-launched Polaris missiles.

347
00:27:49,140 --> 00:27:51,480
But the new weapons had their share of problems.

348
00:27:51,481 --> 00:27:56,380
When you fire a ballistic missile out of a submarine, you do damage to the launch tubes.

349
00:27:56,480 --> 00:27:59,940
You're firing a rocket motor within a very small space.

350
00:28:00,560 --> 00:28:04,840
We eventually figured out that the smarter way to do this is what is called a cold launch,

351
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:10,500
where you use compressed gas to basically blast the missile out of the tube or the silo, if you will, in the submarine.

352
00:28:11,080 --> 00:28:14,860
And once it pops to the surface, then its rocket motor ignites and it takes off.

353
00:28:15,780 --> 00:28:18,820
Now that the Navy had nuclear missiles on submarines,

354
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:24,580
Policymakers realized the Air Force and Navy had to work together on strategic nuclear planning.

355
00:28:24,581 --> 00:28:28,640
What we establish in SAC is an organization called the JSTPS,

356
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:31,820
which stands for Joint Strategic Target Planning Staff,

357
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:36,440
designed to coordinate in a very detailed sense how one would use, in this case,

358
00:28:36,680 --> 00:28:39,700
Air Force nuclear weapons and naval nuclear weapons.

359
00:28:40,220 --> 00:28:44,020
Just as SAC improved the planning and strategy of using nuclear weapons,

360
00:28:44,440 --> 00:28:47,320
so was it improving its physical ability to deliver them.

361
00:28:48,820 --> 00:28:54,740
the ready pad was the offensive center of the sack bomber base it was designed to get bombers

362
00:28:54,741 --> 00:29:01,860
off the ground faster than ever before also known as the alert pad it was separate from the rest of

363
00:29:01,861 --> 00:29:08,740
the base on a raised double-fenced restricted area on the pad planes fully fueled and loaded

364
00:29:08,741 --> 00:29:15,940
with weapons were ready to fly at a moment's notice by 1960 one-third of sax bombers could

365
00:29:15,941 --> 00:29:21,860
be launched within 15 minutes security around the alert pad was some of the tightest in the military

366
00:29:23,140 --> 00:29:26,500
security police kept a watchful eye over all personnel who entered

367
00:29:27,540 --> 00:29:32,340
technicians arrived in pairs and were not allowed out of each other's sight if they lost

368
00:29:33,220 --> 00:29:42,260
track of each other usually a security police that witnessed it would call alert to that and

369
00:29:42,261 --> 00:29:45,920
the technicians would be forced to the ground and searched.

370
00:29:46,720 --> 00:29:51,860
With such high stakes, SAC leadership always had to remain in control of their commands,

371
00:29:52,280 --> 00:29:54,740
even under a full-scale nuclear attack.

372
00:29:55,620 --> 00:30:01,840
With that in mind, on February 3, 1961, SAC initiated Project Looking Glass.

373
00:30:02,860 --> 00:30:06,720
EC-135 aircraft, similar to 707 jetliners,

374
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:10,460
were outfitted with identical command capabilities as SAC headquarters.

375
00:30:10,461 --> 00:30:21,621
The concept that at any time, if Offutt Air Force Base was destroyed, you had an EC-135 with a staff that could command and identify what needed to happen and make

376
00:30:21,622 --> 00:30:30,660
decisions at any time of day or night, really was something that had never happened in any military organization prior to that time.

377
00:30:31,140 --> 00:30:36,860
The term looking glass came from the aircraft's ability to mirror exactly headquarters command.

378
00:30:36,861 --> 00:30:43,860
Nineteen crew members led by a general officer, and including flight crew, communications

379
00:30:43,861 --> 00:30:49,260
experts, tacticians, maintenance and air traffic control people, took to the air for eight

380
00:30:49,261 --> 00:30:51,860
hours at a time.

381
00:30:51,861 --> 00:30:59,740
The system offered redundancy of command, a type of insurance policy against failure.

382
00:30:59,741 --> 00:31:04,180
Project Looking Glass exemplified a term often associated with SAC.

383
00:31:04,181 --> 00:31:05,660
Failsafe.

384
00:31:05,661 --> 00:31:10,840
The SAC command system was spread out, in the case of an EC-135 potentially anywhere

385
00:31:10,841 --> 00:31:16,000
above the United States, and yet so intertwined that if a portion was destroyed, the system

386
00:31:16,001 --> 00:31:17,000
was safe from failure.

387
00:31:17,001 --> 00:31:22,220
For the Russians, Project Looking Glass created another target to worry about, a target that

388
00:31:22,221 --> 00:31:28,940
had the ability to broadcast launch codes to all SAC bases, aircraft, and missile silos.

389
00:31:28,941 --> 00:31:33,160
The EC-135, there was one of those flying at all times.

390
00:31:33,161 --> 00:31:37,160
one would land another one would take off and they would make sure everything was in order and in

391
00:31:37,161 --> 00:31:43,880
place in case of that worst case scenario and that worst case scenario was never closer than on

392
00:31:43,881 --> 00:31:52,520
october 22nd 1962 during the cuban missile crisis the joint chiefs of staff placed the strategic air

393
00:31:52,521 --> 00:31:59,880
command at defcon 2 the highest defense condition ever reached the lowest defcon defcon 5 denotes

394
00:31:59,881 --> 00:32:05,960
normal peacetime operations, while the highest, DEFCON 1, places military forces at maximum

395
00:32:05,961 --> 00:32:06,862
readiness.

396
00:32:06,863 --> 00:32:13,900
At DEFCON 2, SAC had 76 B-52s on 24-hour flights, fully armed, orbiting within

397
00:32:13,901 --> 00:32:16,656
striking distance of their Russian targets.

398
00:32:16,657 --> 00:32:19,540
During the Cuban Missile Crisis, we had B-52s

399
00:32:19,541 --> 00:32:24,600
fully loaded with nukes on two routes, one that went up over the poles and one I flew

400
00:32:24,601 --> 00:32:25,301
on.

401
00:32:25,302 --> 00:32:29,580
We coasted out from North Carolina, flew over to Spain and refueled, went around the

402
00:32:29,581 --> 00:32:32,190
Mediterranean and refuel over Spain again.

403
00:32:32,191 --> 00:32:34,800
At any point in time, we could have pulled

404
00:32:34,801 --> 00:32:38,100
out of our route and gone straight to our targets in the Soviet Union.

405
00:32:39,220 --> 00:32:43,740
Thankfully, the Cuban Missile Crisis ended peacefully and helped change the way both

406
00:32:43,741 --> 00:32:45,829
sides looked at their nuclear arsenals.

407
00:32:45,830 --> 00:32:48,240
It was a wake-up call to the chilling reality

408
00:32:48,241 --> 00:32:51,301
that nuclear weapons could end the world.

409
00:32:51,302 --> 00:32:54,660
From that point on, the nuclear arsenals were

410
00:32:54,661 --> 00:32:59,040
viewed as deterrents as both sides subscribe to the unwritten pact of

411
00:32:59,041 --> 00:33:05,760
mutually assured destruction or MAD. Next, an accident near the North Pole

412
00:33:05,761 --> 00:33:11,220
changes SAC and improvements come to the Workhorse B-52s.

413
00:33:11,221 --> 00:33:17,420
From February of 1961 until July of 1990, SAC had at least one Project

414
00:33:17,421 --> 00:33:22,780
Lookingglass EC-135 Command aircraft flying at all times. The Strategic Air

415
00:33:22,781 --> 00:33:25,467
Command will return on Modern Marvels.

416
00:33:25,468 --> 00:33:29,780
We now return to the Strategic Air Command on Modern Marvels.

417
00:33:32,780 --> 00:33:37,780
Through SAC's entire history, there were no accidental missile launches or nuclear weapons

418
00:33:37,781 --> 00:33:42,113
detonations. Still, there were mishaps.

419
00:33:42,114 --> 00:33:48,780
On January 17, 1966, a B-52 loaded with four nuclear weapons

420
00:33:48,781 --> 00:33:52,940
collided with a KC-135 tanker while refueling over Spain.

421
00:33:53,500 --> 00:33:55,580
Seven men aboard the two aircraft perished

422
00:33:55,581 --> 00:33:58,460
as the planes crashed near the city of Palomares.

423
00:33:59,500 --> 00:34:02,500
Three of the weapons were destroyed without detonating on impact.

424
00:34:03,420 --> 00:34:05,840
One splashed down five miles out in the ocean

425
00:34:05,841 --> 00:34:08,460
and was the object of a nearly three-month search

426
00:34:08,461 --> 00:34:10,700
before being found by a Navy submersible.

427
00:34:12,720 --> 00:34:14,920
Two years after the incident over Spain,

428
00:34:14,921 --> 00:34:18,420
an Air Force bomber crashed at Tule Air Force Base in Greenland.

429
00:34:18,780 --> 00:34:22,900
again carrying four nuclear weapons, all of which were destroyed in the crash.

430
00:34:22,901 --> 00:34:28,440
In the early 60s, SAC implemented an airborne alert program,

431
00:34:28,620 --> 00:34:33,120
basically meaning that there were bombers and tankers that were always in the air,

432
00:34:33,220 --> 00:34:35,020
fully loaded and ready to go.

433
00:34:35,380 --> 00:34:41,980
Between accidents and budget cuts, the program was just too costly, and the program was scrapped.

434
00:34:42,960 --> 00:34:48,060
The day after the Tuli crash, the Pentagon ordered SAC to cease all training flights

435
00:34:48,061 --> 00:34:49,112
with nuclear weapons.

436
00:34:49,113 --> 00:34:52,820
Though one-third of SAC's bombers were still loaded with weapons and ready

437
00:34:52,821 --> 00:34:57,140
to fly at a moment's notice, the rest of the force left their nuclear weapons on the bases.

438
00:34:58,420 --> 00:35:03,800
For SAC, a new era had begun. Unfortunately, it was not an era of peace.

439
00:35:05,920 --> 00:35:10,980
Linebacker II was a mission that combined Air Force, Marine, and Navy aircraft over Vietnam.

440
00:35:10,981 --> 00:35:19,980
SAC's contribution, for 11 days in the winter of 1972, its B-52s rained conventional bombs down on Hanoi and Haiphong.

441
00:35:20,980 --> 00:35:25,547
Many believe SAC's action brought the North Vietnamese back to peace negotiations with the U.S.

442
00:35:25,548 --> 00:35:26,248
in Paris.

443
00:35:27,980 --> 00:35:33,980
But it also showed the Air Force that the concept of strategic bombing worked much better than the reality.

444
00:35:33,981 --> 00:35:34,845
reality.

445
00:35:34,846 --> 00:35:43,600
15 SAC B-52s, nearly half the bombers lost in the Vietnam War, were downed during

446
00:35:43,601 --> 00:35:44,985
the linebacker mission.

447
00:35:44,986 --> 00:35:48,840
We took losses in linebacker that we'd never experienced before.

448
00:35:49,980 --> 00:35:56,440
I've been through this with all the people that were involved, and I think that I speak the truth

449
00:35:56,441 --> 00:35:59,794
when I say it was a very sobering learning process.

450
00:35:59,795 --> 00:36:02,820
While SAC bombers flew difficult missions over

451
00:36:02,821 --> 00:36:03,675
Vietnam. U.S.

452
00:36:03,676 --> 00:36:08,740
missileers began working with the most advanced weapon ever placed in a silo.

453
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:16,340
In the early 70s, the government started work on the MX missile program.

454
00:36:17,940 --> 00:36:25,860
The 71-foot-long four-stage rocket carried 10 350-kiloton MIRV, or multiple independently

455
00:36:25,861 --> 00:36:28,435
targetable re-entry vehicle warheads.

456
00:36:28,436 --> 00:36:32,820
Now a single ICBM could strike 10 enemy targets with precision.

457
00:36:33,380 --> 00:36:36,500
We now have the ability to launch nuclear weapons in a very short time

458
00:36:37,220 --> 00:36:41,140
and most importantly to threaten many more targets than we could in the past,

459
00:36:41,780 --> 00:36:44,260
which changed this nuclear balance in a radical way.

460
00:36:46,340 --> 00:36:49,345
A lot of people think we had overkill.

461
00:36:49,346 --> 00:36:53,220
Overkill has to be put into a rational framework.

462
00:36:53,221 --> 00:36:59,220
Remember, I've got to be able to suffer a major attack and then respond effectively.

463
00:36:59,221 --> 00:37:05,220
The need to respond effectively pushed every facet of the Strategic Air Command.

464
00:37:05,221 --> 00:37:08,220
The B-52s were constantly upgraded.

465
00:37:08,221 --> 00:37:13,220
By the mid-'80s, the interior electronics included low-light cameras and FLIR,

466
00:37:13,221 --> 00:37:15,220
forward-looking infrared sensors,

467
00:37:15,221 --> 00:37:18,220
that allowed the pilots to fly through fog and dead of night

468
00:37:18,221 --> 00:37:21,220
by watching two television monitors in the cockpit.

469
00:37:23,220 --> 00:37:29,360
The Strategic Air Command was rushing forward on all fronts and heading straight into the digital age.

470
00:37:30,860 --> 00:37:38,760
Next, a SAC training exercise pushes all the wrong buttons, and at its technological height, SAC disappears.

471
00:37:41,940 --> 00:37:44,544
Between 1940 and 1996, the U.S.

472
00:37:44,545 --> 00:37:52,440
spent an estimated $5.8 trillion to build, maintain, and deploy nearly 32,000 nuclear weapons.

473
00:37:52,441 --> 00:37:56,440
The Strategic Air Command will return on Modern Marvels.

474
00:37:56,441 --> 00:38:03,440
We now return to the Strategic Air Command on Modern Marvels.

475
00:38:03,441 --> 00:38:09,440
By the late 70s, the Strategic Air Command had become a fine-tuned machine.

476
00:38:09,441 --> 00:38:17,440
The commanders worked diligently to keep their personnel ready to go to war with the most destructive weapons known to man.

477
00:38:17,441 --> 00:38:24,340
This Air Force film shows a training exercise as SAC commanders at headquarters on Offutt

478
00:38:24,341 --> 00:38:31,420
Air Force Base respond to a simulated Russian attack.

479
00:38:31,421 --> 00:38:36,780
This scenario begins as would a real battle situation.

480
00:38:36,781 --> 00:38:41,120
The Joint Chiefs of Staff send a coded message through the National Command Center in Washington,

481
00:38:41,121 --> 00:38:45,120
D.C., letting SAC know of an increased military alert.

482
00:38:45,121 --> 00:38:47,960
Sir, we have an emergency action message.

483
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:49,260
Go ahead and process it.

484
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:54,300
The Commander-in-Chief of the Strategic Air Command, or SYNC SAC,

485
00:38:54,660 --> 00:38:57,040
takes his place overlooking the command center.

486
00:38:57,820 --> 00:39:00,480
As Washington informs the commander of increasing tensions,

487
00:39:00,980 --> 00:39:02,940
he ratchets up his command's response.

488
00:39:04,540 --> 00:39:06,620
Sir, based on the current situation,

489
00:39:07,140 --> 00:39:11,240
I recommend that we disperse forces to ready, standby, and civilian bases.

490
00:39:11,380 --> 00:39:12,080
Do you concur?

491
00:39:13,020 --> 00:39:14,060
Recommendation approved.

492
00:39:14,860 --> 00:39:15,560
Thank you, sir.

493
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:22,920
He first sends bombers and refueling planes from their assigned bases to other military and civilian airports.

494
00:39:23,880 --> 00:39:27,820
Spread out, the airplanes won't be as easy to target if the Soviets should strike.

495
00:39:30,460 --> 00:39:34,740
Teams of mechanics, communications, and security personnel are also dispersed

496
00:39:34,741 --> 00:39:38,360
to look after the aircraft at their new bases of operation.

497
00:39:39,820 --> 00:39:43,280
Additional personnel are scrambled to alternate missile command centers.

498
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:47,300
Now, multiple teams of missileers have the ability to launch the weapons

499
00:39:47,301 --> 00:39:50,760
in the event a Soviet first strike takes out the primary launch center.

500
00:39:54,500 --> 00:40:00,260
As tensions increase, the Joint Chiefs of Staff order an airborne alert as a show of force.

501
00:40:01,100 --> 00:40:05,800
To comply, the Sink-Sack scrambles a portion of his bombers to their forward attack positions.

502
00:40:05,801 --> 00:40:13,560
Over the next few hours, the remaining bomber crews are briefed and missile commands are

503
00:40:13,561 --> 00:40:14,760
placed on heightened alert.

504
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:18,840
Both aerial and ground mobile command personnel are activated.

505
00:40:19,940 --> 00:40:24,780
The additional command units will be used to carry out retaliatory strikes if SAC headquarters

506
00:40:24,781 --> 00:40:25,500
is destroyed.

507
00:40:27,380 --> 00:40:29,260
Then, the unthinkable.

508
00:40:30,380 --> 00:40:34,440
General Ellis, we have indications of multiple ICBM launches threatening North America.

509
00:40:34,441 --> 00:40:38,240
This activity is now displayed on the balcony monitors behind you.

510
00:40:39,960 --> 00:40:42,080
Sir, your decision time is shown here.

511
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:49,440
Launch the force under positive control. I'm going airborne.

512
00:40:49,780 --> 00:40:50,480
Yes, sir.

513
00:40:50,481 --> 00:41:04,481
For safety, the sink sack takes off in a command aircraft.

514
00:41:07,240 --> 00:41:11,100
In the final steps of the exercise, the Russian launch is confirmed.

515
00:41:11,860 --> 00:41:18,460
The president orders a mass retaliation of American nuclear forces, and their Strategic Air Command is given the go code.

516
00:41:18,461 --> 00:41:32,340
Message follows. Tango, 6, 3, Delta, Yankee. Tango, 6, 1, 7, 2.

517
00:41:32,440 --> 00:41:39,720
If this scenario, which was enacted so calmly, had ever played out in real life, the world as we know it probably would have ended.

518
00:41:42,260 --> 00:41:48,080
But at SAC's technological peak, politics, not warfare, caused its demise.

519
00:41:48,460 --> 00:41:55,760
In the late 80s, Mikhail Gorbachev's perestroika led to revolutionary changes in the Soviet Union.

520
00:41:56,940 --> 00:42:00,020
SAC's strength was reduced with a wavering Soviet threat.

521
00:42:00,560 --> 00:42:03,020
Well, I think it happened gradually.

522
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:08,620
Through most of the Cold War, we actually had aircraft in the air all the time.

523
00:42:08,940 --> 00:42:09,980
That's very expensive.

524
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:13,400
So the first thing was to take them off airborne alert.

525
00:42:13,401 --> 00:42:20,200
And then there was the command and control aircraft, which was in the air 24 hours a day.

526
00:42:20,920 --> 00:42:23,040
Do you have to have that up all the time?

527
00:42:23,400 --> 00:42:25,500
So it happened gradually.

528
00:42:27,160 --> 00:42:32,200
The Berlin Wall had fallen, and countless other Soviet satellites were breaking away.

529
00:42:33,380 --> 00:42:34,940
The Cold War had been won.

530
00:42:38,780 --> 00:42:42,940
On June 1st, 1992, SAC was disbanded.

531
00:42:42,941 --> 00:42:50,780
I think the end of the command meant different things to different people, but for those folks who were so invested in that

532
00:42:51,580 --> 00:42:57,740
lifestyle, in that devotion to purpose, in that commitment to excellence, in that understanding and appreciation

533
00:42:58,380 --> 00:43:00,380
that what the command stood for

534
00:43:01,180 --> 00:43:03,660
was peace and that what they had to do

535
00:43:04,380 --> 00:43:11,340
was to preserve that peace through training and through honor and through constantly asking the question, how can we do this better?

536
00:43:11,341 --> 00:43:17,520
When that all of a sudden was gone, I think there are a lot of SAC veterans who would tell you that they lost something.

537
00:43:18,640 --> 00:43:27,420
Others can claim fame, but the Cold War commander's responsibility was to make sure that there was no war and no sacrifice.

538
00:43:28,020 --> 00:43:31,440
We didn't walk the cat back, and we didn't get him into a fight.

539
00:43:32,720 --> 00:43:37,880
Today, bilateral disarmament continues to shrink the size of the United States' nuclear arsenal.

540
00:43:37,881 --> 00:43:45,960
US forces reduced by over 50% still hold a tremendous advantage over the crumbling military of the former Soviet Union.

541
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:52,320
Today's American nuclear forces are overseen by multiple commands within different military branches.

542
00:43:54,280 --> 00:44:04,480
While the targeting and planning of the weapons falls to a relatively small military entity created in 1992, simply called Strategic Command.

543
00:44:04,481 --> 00:44:15,080
But the blueprint from which all the commands work was once the sole domain of the most powerful, ominous, and focused military structure ever created.

544
00:44:15,540 --> 00:44:18,620
The United States Strategic Air Command.

545
00:44:21,100 --> 00:44:25,640
I'm inside the heart and soul of America's air defense giant.

